1
00:00:01,540 --> 00:00:03,960
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:05,160
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:09,400
My name is Catherine and today, Marco, we're trying to make some plans.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:12,100
We're trying to go to a restaurant, correct?

5
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:12,720
That's right.

6
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:20,740
We want to go to a restaurant tonight, but before we have to call them to make sure that there is a space or room available for us.

7
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:21,840
All right.

8
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:28,080
So we're going to hear a conversation between a person at a restaurant who works there and a person who would like to go to that restaurant.

9
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:28,960
Let's take a listen.

10
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,740
Bruno Bistro, how may I help you?

11
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:38,580
Yes, hello.

12
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:40,320
I would like to make a reservation, please.

13
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:41,140
Certainly, sir.

14
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:42,360
For which day and time, please?

15
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:44,080
Tonight at seven.

16
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:47,240
I'm sorry, sir, but we are fully booked tonight until eight.

17
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:50,280
In that case, eight o'clock is fine.

18
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:50,520
Very well.

19
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:51,780
And how many people will attend tonight?

20
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:52,820
Four people.

21
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:56,180
Lastly, may I please know what name I should make the reservation under?

22
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:56,920
Mark.

23
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:00,630
All right, we're back.

24
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:02,450
So let's take a look at Language Takeaway.

25
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:05,990
Language Takeaway.

26
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:11,360
So first up, Marco, we didn't hear the word restaurant in today's dialogue.

27
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:13,600
We heard a different noun.

28
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,280
We heard this bistro.

29
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:16,280
That's right.

30
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:19,280
The name of the restaurant is actually Bruno Bistro.

31
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:24,520
So this restaurant, the type of restaurant this is, it's actually a bistro.

32
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:26,120
That's right.

33
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:33,920
It's a name like Marco or Catherine, but a bistro is actually a special kind of small restaurant.

34
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:34,920
And I believe it comes from France, right?

35
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:35,920
Right.

36
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:36,920
That's right.

37
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:42,440
So it's kind of small and intimate, maybe some candles and lots of wine, but it's not like a big restaurant.

38
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:43,040
It's very romantic.

39
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:43,880
Right, right.

40
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:47,880
Which usually, I found it means that it's more expensive for some reason.

41
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:51,240
Because it's a bistro, it's automatically more expensive.

42
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:53,440
I think maybe in America it's more expensive.

43
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:53,600
Who knows?

44
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:55,380
Maybe in France it's the cheap option.

45
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:55,900
That's right.

46
00:01:56,580 --> 00:02:01,740
Bistro is the kind of place we're at and we'd like to go, so it's probably a popular place.

47
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:03,060
We need to make a reservation.

48
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:04,160
That's right.

49
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,920
So as you see there, we have a verb there, to make.

50
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:09,860
We want to make a reservation.

51
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:13,420
So let's take a look at the noun, a reservation.

52
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:15,640
Reservation.

53
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:15,800
Reservation.

54
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:21,640
So this word means to ask the restaurant to hold your seats.

55
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:25,520
So you say, listen, I would like to come to your restaurant tonight.

56
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:28,760
I don't want to wait when I come, so I will call in advance.

57
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:33,570
I'll call early and ask them to save a table for us.

58
00:02:33,790 --> 00:02:34,190
That's right.

59
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:37,410
So this is called making a reservation.

60
00:02:37,830 --> 00:02:38,430
So it goes together.

61
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:43,050
You call to make a reservation or you want to make a reservation.

62
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:43,890
That's right.

63
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:45,390
So the verb is to make.

64
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:51,590
So I could say, for example, hey Marco, can you please make a reservation for dinner tonight?

65
00:02:51,850 --> 00:02:55,390
I'm afraid there might not be any tables left if we wait.

66
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:56,010
Okay.

67
00:02:56,010 --> 00:02:56,710
Very good.

68
00:02:56,910 --> 00:03:01,750
Now, we can use the verb reserve as well, right?

69
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:02,550
That's right.

70
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:04,950
So reservation is a noun.

71
00:03:05,470 --> 00:03:07,110
To reserve is a verb.

72
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:11,450
So I can say, hey Marco, can you please reserve a table for dinner tonight?

73
00:03:12,510 --> 00:03:12,690
Okay.

74
00:03:12,930 --> 00:03:16,820
So we can say to make a reservation or reserve a table.

75
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:18,960
But you have to say reserve a table.

76
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:22,040
You can't say, I would like to reserve, please.

77
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:24,060
No, you have to reserve something.

78
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:30,940
So if I'm going to a concert, a music concert, I can say, I'd like to reserve two seats for the concert.

79
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,920
But if I'm going to dinner, obviously we sit at a table.

80
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:36,600
So I'm going to reserve a table.

81
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:37,600
All right.

82
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:47,480
Now, moving on to another verb, the waiter or the person in charge there asked us how many people will attend dinner tonight.

83
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:48,440
So how many people will attend?

84
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:50,000
To attend.

85
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:50,580
Okay.

86
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:51,540
This is another verb.

87
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:56,220
To attend means to be there or to come.

88
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:57,660
That's right.

89
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:00,400
So it's actually a nicer way of saying to come.

90
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:03,930
Instead of him saying, how many people will come tonight?

91
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:05,770
He says, how many people will attend?

92
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:06,910
All right.

93
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:10,870
So that's a question for the person making the reservation.

94
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:12,050
How many people total?

95
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:15,280
But it's like you say, very polite.

96
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:20,910
You could say, hey, Marco, how many people do you think will attend your birthday party next week?

97
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:21,950
That's right.

98
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:24,770
So I can say, well, maybe 10 people will attend.

99
00:04:25,330 --> 00:04:26,630
10 people will attend.

100
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:33,400
So attend, quite a simple verb, but just remember that it means to come or to be present at.

101
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:34,400
All right, great.

102
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:39,280
So let's take a look at our dialogue again, and then we'll be back in a bit with Fluency Builder.

103
00:04:45,510 --> 00:04:47,310
Bruno Bistro, how may I help you?

104
00:04:47,670 --> 00:04:48,170
Yes, hello.

105
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:49,900
I would like to make a reservation, please.

106
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:50,700
Certainly, sir.

107
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:51,900
For which day and time, please?

108
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:54,040
Tonight at 7.

109
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:57,040
I'm sorry, sir, but we are fully booked tonight until 8.

110
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,840
In that case, 8 o'clock is fine.

111
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:00,080
Very well.

112
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:01,340
And how many people will attend tonight?

113
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:02,840
Four people.

114
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:05,760
Lastly, may I please know what name I should make the reservation under?

115
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:06,260
Mark.

116
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:11,680
All right, we've picked out three phrases for you.

117
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:13,910
Let's take a look at those now on Fluency Builder.

118
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:17,510
Fluency Builder.

119
00:05:19,470 --> 00:05:25,710
All right, so the first phrase we have on Fluency Builder is actually a phrase you don't want to hear when you're trying to make a reservation.

120
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:32,520
The person at the restaurant, the bistro, said, I'm sorry, we're fully booked until 8.

121
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:33,520
That's right.

122
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:39,390
So a place that is fully booked or maybe a flight that's fully booked means that it's completely full.

123
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:42,870
There are no available seats or available spots or anything.

124
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:45,460
All the tables have been reserved.

125
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:50,560
All right, so this means that we have to change either the day or the time.

126
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:51,100
That's right.

127
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:53,160
It's fully booked.

128
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:56,700
But the nice thing is that after 8 o'clock is not fully booked.

129
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,160
There are free tables after 8.

130
00:05:59,160 --> 00:05:59,300
That's right.

131
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:04,640
And I actually went on to say an interesting phrase to start that sentence.

132
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:07,430
He said, in that case, 8 o'clock is fine.

133
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:08,770
In that case.

134
00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:24,440
So in that case is a great phrase to use when you're having a conversation with someone or a negotiation, because it's something that we say when the situation, okay, like reserving a table is not exactly the way we hoped it would be.

135
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:29,360
So with today's dialogue, we wanted to book a table at 7 o'clock.

136
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:30,180
There was no table.

137
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:37,460
So in that case, in that situation, because it changed from what we thought, we had to book a table at 8 instead.

138
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,000
That's right.

139
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,130
So it's usually a response to information that was given to you.

140
00:06:44,050 --> 00:06:49,890
So they say, oh, I'm sorry, there are no tickets available for the zoo today, only for tomorrow.

141
00:06:50,470 --> 00:06:52,750
In that case, why don't we go tomorrow?

142
00:06:53,150 --> 00:06:53,610
That's right.

143
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:58,800
So I wanted to go today, but the situation changed or there's a problem.

144
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:01,280
In that case, let's change our plans.

145
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:02,180
That's right.

146
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:09,260
And lastly, the waiter asked, may I please know what name I should make the reservation under?

147
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:13,120
What name I should make the reservation under?

148
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:13,980
All right.

149
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,540
Now to make a reservation, we already learned this phrase.

150
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:18,000
It means to reserve a table.

151
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:24,460
Now to make a reservation under a person's name, this is quite different, right?

152
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:31,680
This means that we're going to save a table, reserve a table, but we have to provide a name.

153
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:36,720
So if we say, okay, one table reserved for four, we don't know who.

154
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:40,680
So this is the name that you will give the waiter when you arrive at the restaurant.

155
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:40,880
That's right.

156
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:45,640
So that is your table basically, and that's your reservation.

157
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:52,800
So if you go to the restaurant, you say, hello, I have a reservation under the name of Mark.

158
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:52,940
That's right.

159
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:59,160
I have a reservation under the name Mark, or I have a reservation for Mark at eight o'clock.

160
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:04,500
Now when they ask the question, they're going to say, what name should we make the reservation under?

161
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:09,020
That's because, so just think about it as when they make the reservation, they have this big book.

162
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:12,360
They write your name and underneath they write the time.

163
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:14,560
What name should we make the reservation under?

164
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:15,660
That's right.

165
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:16,260
Okay.

166
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:20,700
So we've taken a look at a lot of vocab and some really good phrases.

167
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:31,120
So let's listen to the dialogue one last time.

168
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:32,700
Bruno Bistro, how may I help you?

169
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Yes, hello.

170
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,280
I would like to make a reservation, please.

171
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:36,100
Certainly, sir.

172
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:37,340
For which day and time, please?

173
00:08:37,780 --> 00:08:39,020
Tonight at seven.

174
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:42,200
I'm sorry, sir, but we are fully booked tonight until eight.

175
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:44,410
In that case, eight o'clock is fine.

176
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:45,450
Very well.

177
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:46,730
And how many people will attend tonight?

178
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:47,790
Four people.

179
00:08:48,290 --> 00:08:51,130
Lastly, may I please know what name I should make the reservation under?

180
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:53,520
Mark.

181
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:56,240
Language takeaway.

182
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:01,520
All right, we're back.

183
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:03,940
So making a reservation, now this is fairly easy.

184
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:09,300
This is basically what you're going to experience if you call any restaurant to make a reservation, right?

185
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:10,280
That's right.

186
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:17,400
And it's really important to know how to respond because I know in other languages, sometimes you reserve seats instead of tables.

187
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,990
And so in English, we reserve or we book a table.

188
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:30,990
Now sometimes you will call a place and you'll say, well, I'd like to make a reservation and they will say, I'm sorry, we don't accept reservations.

189
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:37,720
Yes, this is actually a very frustrating situation, especially if a restaurant or bistro is very popular.

190
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:39,320
Right.

191
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:47,180
Because if they don't accept reservations, we don't make reservations, that means that you have to go to the restaurant and maybe wait in line for a table.

192
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:47,760
That's right.

193
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:50,920
That's called, I think, first come, first served, right?

194
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:52,400
That's right.

195
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:52,780
First come, first serve.

196
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:59,010
So that means that the first people to arrive at the restaurant are the first people to get a table.

197
00:09:59,570 --> 00:10:01,390
So it's smart to arrive early.

198
00:10:01,710 --> 00:10:02,190
That's right.

199
00:10:02,650 --> 00:10:04,250
So that's what happens.

200
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:08,310
Now there are certain restaurants where you can't make a reservation or different.

201
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:10,370
We took a look at a bistro.

202
00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:14,330
There's another one, like for example, very popular in the US, a diner.

203
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:15,530
A diner.

204
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:18,450
Now this is not a place you need to make a reservation for.

205
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:21,810
Normally a diner is a very casual place to eat.

206
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:25,630
You can go at nine o'clock in the morning for breakfast.

207
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:27,430
It's not very fancy at all.

208
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:28,600
Right.

209
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:29,570
And it's open, I think, 24 hours usually.

210
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:31,290
Many of them are open 24 hours.

211
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:45,170
And that's why I say it's not a place you need to worry about a reservation because they're usually popular at certain times, but like seven o'clock for breakfast or, you know, 12 o'clock for lunch, but you can usually get a table.

212
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:46,680
Right.

213
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:47,680
All right.

214
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:52,560
So if you guys have any questions or any other doubts, you can find us at EnglishPod.com and we'll see everyone there.

